<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="ck3-geometry">
  <title>Geometry and cohomology of a cubic fourfold</title>
  <section xml:id="ck3-hypersurface">
    <title>The hypersurface and its polarization</title>
    <p xml:id="ck3-cubic-definition"><idx>cubic fourfold</idx> Let <m>V</m> be a six-dimensional complex vector space. A cubic fourfold is a smooth hypersurface <m>X=V(f)\subset\mathbb P(V)</m> defined by a homogeneous cubic polynomial. Write <m>h=c_1(\mathcal O_X(1))</m>. In particular, <m>\int_X h^4=3</m>, since four general hyperplanes cut the cubic in three points.</p>
    
    <p xml:id="ck3-anticanonical">The canonical bundle follows from the conormal sequence. For a smooth hypersurface of degree three in <m>\mathbb P^5</m>, taking determinants gives
      <md number="no"><mrow>K_X=(K_{\mathbb P^5}\otimes\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^5}(3))|_X=\mathcal O_X(-3).</mrow></md>
      Thus <m>X</m> is Fano of index three. The Lefschetz hyperplane theorem gives <m>\operatorname{Pic}(X)=\mathbb Z\mathcal O_X(1)</m>, so the index is exactly three. The cubic has an ample anticanonical bundle, whereas a K3 surface has trivial canonical bundle. The relation with K3 surfaces must therefore concern another geometric object or a part of cohomology.</p>
    <p>The integral Lefschetz hyperplane theorem gives <m>H^0(X,\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z</m>, <m>H^2(X,\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z h</m>, and vanishing odd cohomology below the middle degree. It gives the corresponding assertions for homology; in particular, <m>H_3(X,\mathbb Z)=0</m>. The universal coefficient theorem then makes <m>H^4(X,\mathbb Z)</m> torsion-free. Poincaré duality determines the groups above the middle degree. Thus all cohomology is torsion-free, all odd groups vanish, and the only even group whose rank is not one is <m>H^4(X,\mathbb Z)</m>. These topological facts and the Jacobian calculation below are treated together in <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-cubics" detail="Chapter 1, Sections 1 and 4"/>.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-integral-hyperplane-powers">The hyperplane powers need not generate the groups in which they lie. Let <m>\eta\in H^6(X,\mathbb Z)</m> be the Poincaré-dual generator characterized by <m>\int_Xh\eta=1</m>. Then <m>h^3=3\eta</m>, since <m>\int_Xh^4=3</m>. Every line <m>\ell\subset X</m> has <m>[\ell]=\eta</m>; the existence of such lines is proved independently in <xref ref="ck3-lines-smooth"/>. In top degree, <m>h^4=3[\mathrm{pt}]</m>. The middle-degree class <m>h^2</m>, by comparison, is primitive as an integral vector: if it were divisible by an integer <m>k\gt1</m>, its square three would be divisible by <m>k^2</m>.</p>
    <aside xml:id="margin-ck3-powers">
      <title>An integral distinction</title>
      <p>The statement that cohomology outside the middle degree is inherited from projective space is safest over <m>\mathbb Q</m>. Over <m>\mathbb Z</m>, the restriction of <m>h^3</m> is three times a generator. This distinction will matter when discriminants of lattices are compared.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="ck3-jacobian-ring">
    <title>Jacobian ring and the Hodge numbers</title>
    <p xml:id="ck3-jacobian-ring-definition"><idx>Jacobian ring</idx> The Hodge numbers can be calculated from the defining equation. Let <m>S=\mathbb C[x_0,\ldots,x_5]</m> with its usual grading, and define the Jacobian ring
      <md number="no"><mrow>R_f=S/(\partial f/\partial x_0,\ldots,\partial f/\partial x_5).</mrow></md>
      Smoothness says that the six partial derivatives have no common zero in projective space. Their affine common zero is therefore the origin. Since the polynomial ring is Cohen–Macaulay, these six quadrics form a regular sequence. The Hilbert series of the quotient is
      <md number="no"><mrow>\sum_{j\geq0}\dim(R_f)_j\,t^j=\frac{(1-t^2)^6}{(1-t)^6}=(1+t)^6.</mrow></md>
      Every coefficient can now be read from the binomial expansion.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-residue-calculation">We use the following special case of Griffiths' residue theorem as an external result: for a smooth cubic hypersurface in <m>\mathbb P^5_{\mathbb C}</m> and <m>0\leq p\leq4</m>, residue induces an isomorphism
<md number="no"><mrow>H^{4-p,p}(X)_{\mathrm{prim}}\simeq(R_f)_{3(p+1)-6}.</mrow></md>
The theorem includes the identification of pole-order relations with the Jacobian ideal <xref ref="bib-ck3-griffiths-residues" detail="Section 10"/>. The compatibility with infinitesimal variation is explained in <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-cubics" detail="Chapter 1, Section 4.5"/>.</p>
    <aside xml:id="margin-ck3-residues">
      <title>Pole order and grading</title>
      <p>Increasing the pole order by one changes the numerator degree by three. The residue theorem identifies the corresponding successive piece of the Hodge filtration with that graded piece of the Jacobian ring. Thus the numerator degree and the Hodge bidegree must be tracked together.</p>
    </aside>
    <p>The grading can be checked directly. Contract <m>dx_0\wedge\cdots\wedge dx_5</m> with the Euler vector field <m>\sum_i x_i\partial/\partial x_i</m> to obtain a homogeneous five-form <m>\Omega</m> of degree six. The rational form <m>P\Omega/f^{p+1}</m> descends to projective space when <m>\deg P=3(p+1)-6</m>. Its residue has the required cohomological degree. This homogeneity requirement explains the index in the residue theorem.</p>
    <p>The indices for <m>p=0,1,2,3,4</m> are <m>-3,0,3,6,9</m>. The negative piece is zero, and the quotient has top degree six. The middle coefficients of <m>(1+t)^6</m> therefore give
      <md number="no"><mrow>(h^{4,0}_{\mathrm{prim}},h^{3,1}_{\mathrm{prim}},h^{2,2}_{\mathrm{prim}},h^{1,3}_{\mathrm{prim}},h^{0,4}_{\mathrm{prim}})=(0,1,20,1,0).</mrow></md>
      The class <m>h^2</m> contributes one additional dimension of type <m>(2,2)</m>. Thus the middle Hodge numbers of <m>X</m> are <m>(0,1,21,1,0)</m>, and its fourth Betti number is twenty-three. The one-dimensional <m>H^{3,1}</m> is the period line that will recur in the K3 comparisons.</p>
    <example xml:id="ck3-fermat-ring">
      <p>For the Fermat cubic <m>f=x_0^3+\cdots+x_5^3</m>, the quotient is
        <md number="no"><mrow>R_f=\mathbb C[x_0,\ldots,x_5]/(x_0^2,\ldots,x_5^2).</mrow></md>
        A basis consists of square-free monomials. There is one constant monomial, twenty degree-three monomials, and one degree-six monomial <m>x_0x_1\cdots x_5</m>. This gives the primitive Hodge numbers directly. The calculation computes dimensions for every smooth cubic because the regular-sequence Hilbert series is independent of the coefficients.</p>
    </example>
    <p>The distinction between dimensions and structures is already visible here. A period line is a one-dimensional subspace of a fixed complex vector space after a local topological identification of the family. It can move while all dimensions remain constant. The Jacobian description makes that movement computable by differentiating the rational forms with respect to the coefficients of <m>f</m>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="ck3-cohomology-lattices">
    <title>Primitive and transcendental cohomology</title>
    <p xml:id="ck3-primitive"><idx>primitive cohomology</idx> Write <m>L_X=H^4(X,\mathbb Z)</m> and equip it with the cup-product pairing <m>(a,b)_X=\int_X ab</m>. We write <m>\oplus</m> for orthogonal direct sums of lattices. The primitive lattice is
      <md number="no"><mrow>L_X^0=(h^2)^\perp=\{a\in L_X:(a,h^2)_X=0\}.</mrow></md>
      Over <m>\mathbb Q</m> there is an orthogonal decomposition <m>L_{X,\mathbb Q}=\mathbb Q h^2\oplus L_{X,\mathbb Q}^0</m>. The projection of a class <m>a</m> to the primitive part is <m>a-\frac{(a,h^2)_X}{3}h^2</m>. The denominator explains why this rational decomposition need not be an integral direct sum.</p>
    <p>We will use a specific integral lattice description of cubic cohomology. For every smooth complex cubic fourfold, the pair <m>(L_X,h^2)</m> is isometric to
<md number="no"><mrow>\bigl(E_8^{\oplus2}\oplus U^{\oplus2}\oplus I_{3,0},(1,1,1)\bigr).</mrow></md>
Here <m>E_8</m> and <m>A_2</m> have positive definite forms, and the displayed vector belongs to <m>I_{3,0}</m>. In particular, <m>h^2</m> is characteristic, <m>L_X</m> has signature <m>(21,2)</m>, and
<md number="no"><mrow>L_X^0\simeq E_8^{\oplus2}\oplus U^{\oplus2}\oplus A_2.</mrow></md>
Thus <m>L_X^0</m> is even of signature <m>(20,2)</m> and discriminant three, and <m>\mathbb Zh^2\oplus L_X^0</m> has index three in <m>L_X</m>. This description, including the distinguished vector, is the result of Hassett used below <xref ref="bib-ck3-hassett-special" detail="Proposition 2.1.2"/>; compare <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-cubics" detail="Chapter 1, Section 1.5 and Chapter 6, Section 5.2"/>.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-transcendental"><idx>transcendental cohomology</idx> The integral Hodge lattice is <m>A_X=L_X\cap H^{2,2}(X)</m>. Its orthogonal complement <m>T_X=A_X^\perp\subset L_X</m> is the transcendental lattice. Since <m>h^2\in A_X</m>, one has <m>T_X\subset L_X^0</m>. If <m>r=\operatorname{rank}A_X</m>, then <m>\operatorname{rank}T_X=23-r</m>. When <m>A_X=\mathbb Zh^2</m>, primitive and transcendental cohomology coincide. Additional integral Hodge classes reduce the rank of <m>T_X</m>, while <m>L_X^0</m> still has rank twenty-two. The very general and special loci are described in <xref ref="ck3-hassett"/>.</p>
    <aside xml:id="margin-ck3-primitive-transcendental">
      <title>Two orthogonal complements</title>
      <p>Primitive cohomology is orthogonal to the single class <m>h^2</m>. Transcendental cohomology is orthogonal to every rational Hodge class of type <m>(2,2)</m>. The twenty-dimensional complex space <m>H^{2,2}_{\mathrm{prim}}</m> need not contain a nonzero rational vector.</p>
    </aside>
    <example xml:id="ck3-plane-primitive">
      <p>If <m>X</m> contains a plane <m>P</m>, then <m>([P],h^2)_X=1</m>. Its rational projection to <m>L_X^0</m> is <m>[P]-h^2/3</m>, and <m>3[P]-h^2</m> is a nonzero integral Hodge class in primitive cohomology. Thus the plane supplies an algebraic direction beyond <m>h^2</m>. The normal-bundle computation and the discriminant-eight intersection matrix are given in <xref ref="ck3-surfaces-and-discriminants"/>.</p>
    </example>
    <p xml:id="ck3-tate-convention"><idx>Tate twist</idx> The weight-two Hodge structure <m>L_X^0(1)</m> has Hodge numbers <m>(1,20,1)</m>. In comparisons with weight-two polarized structures, we equip it with the negative of the cubic cup form, of signature <m>(2,20)</m>. This pairing convention will also be used for the Abel–Jacobi and Mukai comparisons.</p>
    <aside xml:id="margin-ck3-k3-type">
      <title>K3 type</title>
      <p>K3 type does not assert realization as the second cohomology of a projective K3 surface. Such a surface has an ample integral <m>(1,1)</m> class; the transcendental part of a very general cubic has no such class.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="ck3-moduli-and-rationality">
    <title>Deformations and rationality</title>
    <p>A cubic polynomial in six variables has <m>\binom83=56</m> coefficients. Scalar multiples define the same hypersurface, so smooth cubic equations form a nonempty Zariski open subset of <m>\mathbb P^{55}</m>. Projective coordinate changes form <m>\operatorname{PGL}(6)</m>, of dimension thirty-five. Their quotient has dimension twenty. The Jacobian ring explains this subtraction intrinsically: the infinitesimal change of equation produced by a linear change of variables belongs to the degree-three Jacobian ideal.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-moduli-dimension">The six quadratic partial derivatives have no syzygy with linear coefficients, because the first syzygies of a regular sequence of quadrics are generated by the Koszul relations with quadratic coefficients. Their products with the six coordinates therefore span a thirty-six-dimensional subspace of <m>S_3</m>. Euler's identity <m>3f=\sum_i x_i(\partial f/\partial x_i)</m> includes the rescaling direction in that subspace. The quotient <m>(R_f)_3</m> has dimension <m>56-36=20</m> and is the tangent space of deformations modulo coordinate change. The normal and Euler sequences identify it with <m>H^1(X,\mathcal T_X)</m>, where <m>\mathcal T_X</m> is the tangent sheaf. Finite automorphisms can produce quotient singularities in the coarse moduli space, but they do not change its dimension.</p>
    <p>The quotient by the degree-three Jacobian ideal therefore describes actual deformation directions. Differentiating the residue forms computes their effect on the Hodge structure. We carry out that derivative, and distinguish its local consequence from global Torelli, in <xref ref="ck3-torelli-local"/>.</p>
    <p>The parameter space of smooth cubic equations is connected. Any two smooth cubic fourfolds can therefore be joined in a smooth projective family, possibly after restricting to a connected sequence of parameter curves. This explains why their Betti and Hodge numbers agree. It does not make their period lines or their rational Hodge lattices equal. The locations at which extra rational classes acquire type <m>(2,2)</m> will define the special loci considered later.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-rationality-notions">Smooth complex cubic fourfolds are unirational. A dominant parametrization may have degree greater than one, so unirationality alone does not establish rationality. The unirational constructions are discussed in <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-cubics" detail="Chapter 1, Section 5.3 and Chapter 2, Section 1.5"/>. The K3 structures studied here compare cohomology, varieties of lines, and categories; a rationality conclusion requires further geometry.</p>
    <aside xml:id="margin-ck3-generality">
      <title>General and very general</title>
      <p>Conditions on rational Hodge classes involve countably many possible classes. This is why the statements below often require a very general point, rather than a point in one nonempty Zariski open subset.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>
</chapter>